Write back what execution stored into a memory that could not be flattened.

Patch 0001 evals a module whose memory cannot be flattened but refuses every
store into it, and patch 0010 extends that to the start function, because the
one way `applyMemoryToModule` knows to write memory back is to dump the whole
image into a single segment at offset 0. A C module's `_initialize` stores the
flag that it has run before anything else, so a GC program merged with one --
two memories, passive segments, `array.new_data` -- was evalled not at all.

Writing back needs no flattening. Take each memory as its active segments
seeded it, before execution first stores into it, and on every application
compare it with the memory now: each run of changed bytes becomes an active
segment of its own, added after every other, so that instantiation applies it
last and over whatever was there. The module's segments are left alone, which
keeps a passive one, one code names, and one in another memory what they were.
The previous application's segments are replaced, not added to.

